    gallery: ["https://my.rotaract3292.org/storage/report_images/1717518586-3e30f7ac-7d6a-480a-87eb-ed0a6f25bb61.jpg", "https://my.rotaract3292.org/storage/report_images/1717518586-5b53b197-7ce2-4c43-8a42-5d60ab009179.jpg", "https://my.rotaract3292.org/storage/report_images/1717518586-595e5e8a-a02d-484c-9dc7-695ec7329781.jpg"],
    excerpt: "knowledge about every BOD",
    body: "The Rotaract Club of Metro City actively participated in the Zonal Club Officer Training Seminar (COTS), which encompassed Zone XI, XII, and XIII. This collaborative session brought together club officials from across these zones, providing tailored training by district trainers based on their respective positions within the clubs.\n\nThe primary objective of this training seminar was to empower the Board of Directors (BODs) with clear roles and responsibilities, enabling them to effectively and efficiently carry out their duties throughout their tenure. District trainers delivered comprehensive guidance on governance practices, project management, membership engagement, and other essential areas pertinent to their roles within the club.\n\nA highlight of the seminar was the president section, where Rtr. Dinesh Gaire served as the speaker, offering insights and strategies for effective club leadership. His expertise and guidance further enriched the learning experience, equipping presidents with practical tools to lead their clubs with confidence and vision.\n\nThe seminar served as a pivotal opportunity for club officials to enhance their leadership skills, deepen their understanding of Rotary principles, and align their efforts with the broader goals of Rotaract. By equipping BODs with the necessary tools and knowledge, the training aimed to foster competent and proactive leadership within each participating club.\n\nThe Rotaract Club of Metro City acknowledges the invaluable support and mentorship provided by district trainers during this session. The collaborative learning environment not only strengthened individual capabilities but also promoted unity and collaboration among clubs across the zonal boundaries.",
